data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core 9 (.NET 9), the Cosmos DB provider **deprecated** synchronous server operations and threw `InvalidOperationException` by default — but offered an opt-in flag to re-enable sync-over-async behavior. That flag carried forward into .NET 10.

**In .NET 11 (EF Core 11), the sync-over-async opt-in is removed entirely.** All four of your methods will throw `InvalidOperationException` at runtime with no workaround.

## What to change

Convert every synchronous call to its `async` counterpart:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

| Sync (broken in .NET 11) | Async replacement |
|---|---|
| `ToList()` | `await ToListAsync()` |
| `First()` | `await FirstAsync()` |
| `Count()` | `await CountAsync()` |
| `SaveChanges()` | `await SaveChangesAsync()` |

- `Add()` stays synchronous — it only tracks the entity in memory; no I/O occurs until `SaveChangesAsync()`.
- You'll also need to propagate `async/await` up through your callers (controllers, services, etc.).
- Remove any `EnableSyncOverAsync` / sync-over-async configuration — it's no longer recognized and may cause a startup error.
